WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.600
The shared responsibility model explains which security duties are handled by the cloud

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.700
provider and which duties remain with the customer.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.840
It is one of the most important ideas in cloud security because it prevents two common

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.020
mistakes:

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.400
assuming the provider secures everything,

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:20.260
or assuming the customer must manage every layer the same way as an on-premises data

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:21.360
center.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:22.540
In general,

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.960
the provider secures the underlying cloud platform. That includes major parts of the

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.620
physical facilities,

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.360
hardware, foundational networking, and service infrastructure.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:38.540
The customer secures how cloud services are configured and used. That includes identities,

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:44.200
roles, policies, data classification, access decisions, network exposure,

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.060
workload configuration, logging choices, monitoring, and incident response.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.000
The exact boundary depends on the service. Infrastructure services give the customer more

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.540
control and more responsibility.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.680
If a team runs virtual machines,

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.740
it usually needs to think about operating system configuration, patching,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.480
host firewall settings, installed software, workload identity, secrets, logs,

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.300
and backup behavior.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.720
Managed services shift some operational work to the provider,

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.180
but they do not remove customer responsibility.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.320
A managed database may reduce the need to manage the database host,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.980
but the customer still needs to control access, protect data,

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.480
configure network reachability, manage keys, review logs, and understand retention.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.660
Shared responsibility does not mean shared confusion. Each system should have clear

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:35.680
ownership.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:40.540
Someone should know who owns the resource, who owns the data, who can change access,

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.400
who reviews logs, who handles exceptions,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.740
and who responds if a risky configuration appears.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.480
In many organizations, responsibility is split across platform teams, application teams,

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.180
security teams, and governance teams.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.560
That can work well, but only if the boundaries are documented and practiced.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.580
A useful review question is, what could the provider reasonably know or control,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.720
and what can only the customer decide?

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:10.700
The provider cannot know which identity should have access to a business dataset.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.480
The provider cannot know whether a storage bucket contains public documentation or

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.220
confidential records.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.080
The provider cannot decide which application port should be internet-facing for a specific

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:23.180
service.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.800
Those are customer decisions, even when the provider offers tools to implement them.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:33.300
Treat the shared responsibility model as a map for ownership. For every cloud resource,

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.640
identify the service model, the provider-managed layer,

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:41.340
the customer-managed configuration, and the team accountable for daily operation.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.240
The model should reduce gaps,

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:48.340
not create excuses. Secure cloud work starts when each team understands both the

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.400
provider's role and its own.
